<div class="article-body prompt-article space-y-14" style="margin-top: 3rem;"><section>
<figure class="my-10 flex flex-col items-center gap-3"><img src="/my-portfolio/images/blog/structured-prompts-hero.svg" alt="A confused robot reads a vague request while a happy robot follows a clearly structured prompt." class="article-diagram rounded-xl w-full" loading="eager" decoding="async"/><figcaption class="article-caption font-mono text-xs text-center">Same robot, better brief.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">You type “review this code.” The model returns a cheerful paragraph, three naming suggestions, and congratulations on your excellent use of functions.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">You wanted it to catch an authorization bug.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">The model had the code. What it did not have was your definition of a useful review: focus on security, identify concrete evidence, ignore cosmetic suggestions, and return findings your application can display.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">That gap is where structured prompts help. They turn an implied agreement into an explicit brief. They do not make a model infallible, but they make the task easier to inspect, reuse, and test. For developers, that should sound pleasantly familiar.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">What is a structured prompt?</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong class="article-strong">structured prompt</strong> organizes a request into recognizable parts: the task, relevant context, source material, constraints, examples, and expected output. Those parts might use Markdown headings, XML-style tags, a reusable template, or a combination.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">There is no sacred syntax. A prompt with three useful headings can be more effective than a cathedral of tags surrounding an unclear request.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Imagine walking into a restaurant and shouting “FOOD!” toward the kitchen. You have expressed a desire, technically. You have not specified the dish, allergies, portion, or whether the meal is for the person currently waving a credit card.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">An order ticket makes the request actionable: one mushroom risotto, no cheese, table seven. A boot served in a bowl would still be unacceptable, but at least everyone can point to the ticket and identify what went wrong.</p>
<figure class="my-10 flex flex-col items-center gap-3"><img src="/my-portfolio/images/blog/structured-prompts-restaurant.svg" alt="A diner shouting at a kitchen receives a boot in a bowl; a written order ticket produces the requested dish." class="article-diagram rounded-xl w-full" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption class="article-caption font-mono text-xs text-center">“Food!” is a mood. An order ticket is a specification.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Structured prompting is that ticket. It reduces ambiguity about what to do and gives you something concrete to evaluate afterward.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">The anatomy: six useful building blocks</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Think of a prompt as a small kit of LEGO bricks:</p>
<ul class="space-y-4"><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Role:</strong> the perspective or expertise the task needs. “Review this as a backend security reviewer” gives a focus; “you are the greatest genius alive” mostly gives the robot a LinkedIn bio.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Context:</strong> the audience, environment, and relevant background. A Node.js API for an internal tool has different constraints from a public browser extension.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Task:</strong> the specific action and deliverable. Prefer “identify authorization risks” to “look at this.”</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Examples:</strong> small demonstrations of the behavior you want, especially for ambiguous labels or writing style.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Constraints:</strong> boundaries, priorities, and what to do when information is missing.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Output format:</strong> the shape a human or program will consume: a table, a short explanation, or a JSON object.</span></li></ul>
<figure class="my-10 flex flex-col items-center gap-3"><img src="/my-portfolio/images/blog/structured-prompts-anatomy.svg" alt="LEGO bricks labeled role, context, task, examples, constraints, and output format combine into a complete prompt." class="article-diagram rounded-xl w-full" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption class="article-caption font-mono text-xs text-center">Use the bricks the task needs. You do not need to build a castle every time.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">You rarely need all six. A simple rewrite may need only a task and a tone. A production extraction pipeline usually needs explicit missing-data behavior and an output contract. Add structure to resolve ambiguity, not to satisfy a ceremonial checklist.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Five types you can mix</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">These are useful patterns, not mutually exclusive scientific categories.</p>
<ul class="prompt-patterns space-y-4"><li class="prompt-pattern"><h3 class="article-strong font-mono font-bold mb-3">Pattern: Delimited / XML–Markdown</h3><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">What it organizes:</strong> Sections and boundaries between instructions and material</p><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Good fit:</strong> Reviewing code or summarizing documents</p></li><li class="prompt-pattern"><h3 class="article-strong font-mono font-bold mb-3">Pattern: Template-based</h3><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">What it organizes:</strong> Stable instructions with variable inputs</p><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Good fit:</strong> Repeated product summaries or support triage</p></li><li class="prompt-pattern"><h3 class="article-strong font-mono font-bold mb-3">Pattern: Example-driven / few-shot</h3><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">What it organizes:</strong> Demonstrations of the desired mapping</p><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Good fit:</strong> Classification, extraction, and house style</p></li><li class="prompt-pattern"><h3 class="article-strong font-mono font-bold mb-3">Pattern: Schema-constrained output</h3><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">What it organizes:</strong> Machine-readable response structure</p><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Good fit:</strong> Application integrations</p></li><li class="prompt-pattern"><h3 class="article-strong font-mono font-bold mb-3">Pattern: Step-based / chained</h3><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">What it organizes:</strong> Work broken into stages</p><p class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Good fit:</strong> Tasks with distinct intermediate deliverables</p></li></ul>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A template can contain XML sections, include examples, request a schema, and run as one stage of a chain. Pick the combination that makes your task understandable.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Also distinguish a <strong class="article-strong">structured prompt</strong> from <strong class="article-strong">structured output</strong>. The first organizes the input. The second organizes the response. Clear instructions can request JSON, while supported API features can constrain generation to a schema. Those are different levels of control.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 1: write the acceptance criteria first</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Before choosing tags, decide what a good answer must contain. “Helpful” is difficult to test. “Every finding identifies a location, evidence, and an impact” is much more useful.</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 1: write the acceptance criteria first · text</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[0]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">That last instruction matters. If you demand exactly five bugs, you are giving the model a quota that may compete with accuracy. Security review should not operate like a sales leaderboard.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 2: separate instructions from source material</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Use section labels that explain the job of each block. XML-style tags are useful when several kinds of material appear together; Markdown headings are often enough for simpler tasks.</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 2: separate instructions from source material · xml</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[1]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">The tags label meaning; they are not special model commands. Anthropic's <a class="prompt-source" href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents">context engineering guidance</a> recommends distinct sections while emphasizing relevant, concise context.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="article-strong">Delimiters are not a security boundary.</strong> An untrusted document can contain malicious instructions or your closing tag. Keep trusted rules separate from untrusted content using the message roles supported by your API, handle delimiters deliberately, and enforce permissions in application code. No angle bracket should hold the keys to production.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 3: define a real output contract</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">“Return JSON” leaves plenty unanswered. Which keys? Which values? What happens if the text does not contain a deadline?</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Start by spelling out those decisions:</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 3: define a real output contract · text</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[2]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">An acceptable result for “Prepare the demo” would be:</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 3: define a real output contract · json</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[3]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">For an application, express the shape as a schema too:</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 3: define a real output contract · json</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[4]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">This is a schema fragment, not a complete API request. Where available, supply it through the provider's native structured-output configuration rather than only pasting it into the prompt. Supported schema features vary. OpenAI's <a class="prompt-source" href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs">Structured Outputs guide</a> describes schema adherence and handling refusals and incomplete responses.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A valid object can still contain a wrong answer. Parse it, validate its shape, and check the business rules and evidence. JSON is a container, not a truth serum.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 4: teach the edge cases with examples</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Few-shot prompting supplies a few input/output demonstrations. It is especially useful when your labels sound obvious until two teammates disagree about them.</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 4: teach the edge cases with examples · text</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[5]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Examples should match your written rules. Include a borderline or missing-information case, not just three easy victories. Keep examples short enough that their pattern is visible, and evaluate on different inputs. Otherwise you have taught the model your test answers and celebrated its excellent memory.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 5: turn recurring work into a typed template</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">If a prompt runs every day, stop rebuilding it by hand. Keep instructions stable and make the changing inputs explicit.</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 5: turn recurring work into a typed template · typescript</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[6]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">TypeScript catches some mistakes in your own code. Runtime validation still matters for external inputs. JSON serialization escapes the string into a defined representation; it does <strong class="article-strong">not</strong> neutralize prompt injection. In an API integration, keep trusted instructions in the appropriate instruction message and send source data separately.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Version the template with the application. When behavior changes, a diff should explain which rule changed. That is easier to debug than “someone adjusted the vibes.”</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 6: split work when the stages need different checks</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A request to extract facts, resolve contradictions, evaluate risks, and write a polished announcement is asking one call to juggle four jobs while riding a unicycle.</p>
<figure class="my-10 flex flex-col items-center gap-3"><img src="/my-portfolio/images/blog/structured-prompts-chain.svg" alt="A sweating juggler struggles with one giant prompt while an assembly line performs extract, analyze, and write in separate stages." class="article-diagram rounded-xl w-full" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption class="article-caption font-mono text-xs text-center">Sometimes the robot needs a workflow, not another juggling ball.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A chain makes intermediate results available for inspection:</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 6: split work when the stages need different checks · text</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[7]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A numbered list in one prompt is a step-based instruction. A <strong class="article-strong">prompt chain</strong> uses separate calls, passing selected outputs between them. You do not need the model's private reasoning: ask for observable artifacts such as extracted facts, evidence, or a concise decision rationale.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Chaining adds latency, token costs, and failure points. An extraction mistake can travel downstream wearing a freshly ironed shirt. Validate intermediate artifacts, preserve source references, and stop or route uncertain cases for review. Use a chain when those checkpoints earn their complexity.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Strategy 7: evaluate the prompt like an interface</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Keep a small set of representative inputs: a typical case, missing data, conflicting evidence, unexpected language, and text that tries to override the task.</p>
<div class="code-block rounded-xl overflow-x-auto mb-8"><div class="code-header flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs"><span>Strategy 7: evaluate the prompt like an interface · text</span></div><pre class="code-pre p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{promptSamples[8]}</code></pre></div>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Check parsing and required fields automatically. Review factual support and usefulness separately. Run comparisons with the same model and relevant settings, and repeat cases when variability matters. Change one major instruction at a time so improvements have an explanation.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">A prompt that passes one friendly example is a promising draft. It has not earned a production badge yet.</p>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Common pitfalls: beautifully formatted confusion</h2>
<ul class="space-y-4"><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Too much context:</strong> a complete repository dump can bury the relevant handler. Supply what the task needs and explain dependencies that matter.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Conflicting rules:</strong> “be exhaustive” and “use one sentence” need a priority or a narrower task.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Role theater:</strong> assigning a prestigious persona does not supply missing facts or domain validation.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Forced certainty:</strong> give the model a legitimate way to report missing information, ambiguity, or unsupported claims.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Schema worship:</strong> a field called <code>verified</code> does not verify anything. Your code and evidence checks must do that work.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3"><span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">▸</span><span class="article-p leading-relaxed"><strong class="article-strong">Infinite prompt growth:</strong> after every failure, resist adding another paragraph forever. Simplify, identify the failure mode, and test a targeted change.</span></li></ul>
</section><section>
<h2 class="font-mono font-bold text-xl mb-4">Give the model a brief you can review</h2>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Start with the task and acceptance criteria. Add the context that changes the answer, label source material, show tricky examples, and define the output your next step actually needs.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">Then test the result. Structure is valuable because it makes expectations visible—to the model, to your teammates, and to the future version of you debugging this at 11 p.m.</p>
<p class="article-p leading-relaxed mb-4">You do not need a magical incantation. You need a decent order ticket, a way to check the dish, and the confidence to send the boot back to the kitchen.</p>
</section></div>
